<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wulfhere of Mercia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 675</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wulfhere was king of Mercia from 658 to 675, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a>. He threw off Northumbrian rule and made Mercia the dominant power in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, controlling Surrey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and Essex. A Christian king, he gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a> to the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> king Aethelwealh and supported the spread of the church in his lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wulfhere-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Wulfhere of Mercia" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sjwells53 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chad_Peada_Wulfhere_at_Lichfield.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wulfhere was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a>, who was killed on 15 November 655 at the Battle of Winwaed, fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu of Northumbria</a>. Oswiu divided Mercia, keeping the north under his own control and giving the south to Penda's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peada">Peada</a>, who was murdered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> 656. Oswiu then ruled all Mercia himself. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> describes Wulfhere as a youth at his accession, so he was probably in his middle teens. In 658 three Mercian leaders, Immin, Eafa and Eadbert, rebelled against the Northumbrians, and according to Bede they had kept Wulfhere in hiding; when the revolt succeeded he became king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Wulfhere was the first Christian king of all Mercia, though the date and circumstances of his conversion are unknown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says that, free under their own king, the Mercians gave willing allegiance to Christ. Wulfhere invited Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a> to Mercia in 667-69 when a bishop's services were needed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> says he endowed the monastery at Medeshamstede, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a>. He married Eormenhild, a daughter of King Eorcenberht of Kent.</p><p>In 661 he harried Ashdown in West Saxon territory and raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a>, which he gave, with the territory of the Meonware, to his godson King Æthelwealh of the South Saxons. He ordered the priest Eoppa to baptise the islanders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where Wulfhere sold the see to Wine between 665 and 668, lay in the lands of the East Saxons, whose kings Sigehere and Sæbbi ruled under him. A charter of 673-675 shows him confirming a grant in Surrey. By the early 660s he was the effective overlord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a>, though not of Northumbria. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tribal-hidage">Tribal Hidage</a> may date from his reign, though other origins have been suggested.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 674 Wulfhere attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecgfrith-of-northumbria">Ecgfrith of Northumbria</a>. According to Stephen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripon">Ripon</a>'s Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>, he had stirred up all the southern nations against the Northumbrians, but Ecgfrith defeated him, forced him to surrender Lindsey and made him pay tribute. In 675 Æscwine, one of the kings of the West Saxons, fought him at Biedanheafde; where the battle was and who won it are not known. Wulfhere died later in 675, probably of disease, in his mid-thirties.</p><p>His brother Æthelred succeeded him and reigned for nearly thirty years. Æthelred recovered Lindsey a few years after his accession but could not keep the domination of the south that Wulfhere had achieved. Modern historians consider that the rise of Mercia to primacy began in Wulfhere's reign. Eormenhild is thought to have become abbess of Ely. Stephen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripon">Ripon</a> described Wulfhere as a man of proud mind and insatiable will.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wulfhere of Mercia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A rising of Mercian nobles in 658 placed Penda's son <b>Wulfhere</b> on the throne, and his brother Æthelred defeated Ecgfrith of Northumbria at the Battle of the Trent in 679, ending Northumbrian claims south of the Humber and recovering Lindsey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The only known ruler is Frithuwold, a sub-king under <b>Wulfhere</b> of the Kingdom of Mercia, who around 672 to 674 endowed Chertsey Abbey, founded by Bishop Eorcenwald.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-surrey">Kingdom of Surrey</a></li><li><blockquote>In 661 <b>Wulfhere</b> of the Kingdom of Mercia gave the island to the South Saxon king Aethelwealh.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-isle-of-wight">Kingdom of the Isle of Wight</a></li></ul></section>
